WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.740
A trillion-mile gas streamer is tilting the planet-forming disk around a young triple-star, showing

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.040
how material from outside can reshape entire systems.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.420
This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 154.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:20.680
Alma observations captured a narrow stream of gas stretching 0.2 light-years into the gigawatts

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:21.900
Orionis system.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:27.660
The streamer appears to be feeding material into the young triple-star system from surrounding

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.120
clouds.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.560
This inflow is thought to be responsible for warping the outer planet-forming ring.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.780
The data provide the clearest view yet of how external material can alter disk orientation.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:45.540
Follow-up observations will track whether the streamer continues to deliver mass over time.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:51.860
From one striking example of external influence on young systems, we turn to a mission tracking

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.280
how heat escapes from our own planet's most sensitive regions.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:01.240
NASA's pre-fire mission has produced animations showing seasonal temperature changes at both

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.740
poles over two years.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:08.780
The data reveal how heat escapes from Earth's surface through the atmosphere in polar regions.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.400
Instruments measure far infrared radiation that was previously difficult to observe from

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:14.800
space.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.280
The observations help refine climate models that predict ice loss and atmospheric circulation.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.720
Continued measurements will track how these patterns evolve year to year.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.380
While pre-fire watches heat leaving Earth, another space telescope has been looking back

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.360
at the galaxies that once cleared the early universe.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.540
Spectra of more than 1,400 galaxies indicate that a small subset with high escape fractions

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.760
of ionizing photons drove reionization.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.640
The James Webb Space Telescope data show these galaxies had the right properties to clear

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.360
neutral hydrogen from the intergalactic medium.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.760
The result challenges earlier assumptions that a much larger population was required.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.060
The paper was posted to Adiruk on July 24th.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.200
Additional JWST observations will test whether this pattern holds across other fields.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.000
From the early universe, we move to new maps of our own galaxy's structure and chemistry.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:22.740
SDSSV's 20th data release includes expanded spectroscopic observations of stars throughout

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.520
the Milky Way.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:29.920
The Milky Way mapper component now covers a much larger volume of the galactic disc and halo.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.020
Astronomers can use the new spectra to trace chemical abundances and stellar motions at

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:36.960
greater distances.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.900
The release adds thousands of new targets observed with multiple instruments.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.820
Future data releases will further increase the density of the map.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:51.600
While ground-based surveys chart the Milky Way, a spacecraft closer to home is preparing

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.800
its cameras for the Trojan asteroids.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.380
The spacecraft's instruments are undergoing calibration checks before the first Trojan asteroid encounters.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:06.060
Lucy will fly past multiple asteroids in Jupiter's leading and trailing swarms.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:11.440
Early imaging data will confirm the cameras can resolve surface features at the plan

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:12.080
distances.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:17.020
The mission team is adjusting exposure settings based on the latest test results.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.800
These tests ensure the spacecraft can capture both color and stereo images during the 2027

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:24.460
flybys.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:30.620
From asteroid flybys, we shift to a new observatory already delivering wide field views

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:31.780
of the southern sky.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:41.420
The image shows a wide field view of the southern sky captured during early operations of the

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.860
thousands of galaxies and stars across a single exposure.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:51.180
The observatory's rapid scanning capability will eventually map the entire visible sky every

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:51.720
few nights.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.960
This particular field highlights the telescope's ability to detect faint moving objects.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:03.300
Data from these early images are already being used to refine survey strategies.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:10.440
From new survey capabilities, we turn to laboratory work preparing hardware for the dusty lunar surface.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:16.840
Engineers at Johnson Space Center are testing equipment against sharp, abrasive lunar dust that

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:17.900
clings to surfaces.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:24.440
The facility simulates the moon's environment to verify that landers and rovers can operate reliably.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:29.800
Dust mitigation techniques include specialized coatings and seals under development.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:35.660
This work directly supports the Artemis program's goal of sustainable surface operations.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.020
Hardware that passes these tests will move to flight qualification.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.740
Remember, we covered Artemis yesterday.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:49.060
Today's tests move the crude landing schedule forward by qualifying hardware for sustained surface

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:49.720
operations.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:56.520
From surface operations, we look at the power systems needed for sustained exploration beyond Earth orbit.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:02.280
Idaho National Laboratory is developing nuclear power systems for deep space missions.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:08.720
The work focuses on compact reactors that can supply electricity where solar power is insufficient.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:15.080
Engineers are advancing fuel forms and heat conversion technologies for long duration operation.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:20.860
These systems would support crude habitats and science instruments on the moon and Mars.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.860
Testing continues on materials that withstand years of radiation exposure.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:30.900
From power systems, we move to public efforts shaping future lunar rules.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:37.560
The initiative invites public input on protecting lunar sites and resources during future exploration.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:43.420
It focuses on balancing scientific access with preservation of historically significant

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:49.780
areas on the moon. Organizers aim to gather perspectives from diverse communities before finalizing

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:55.580
guidelines. The project will feed into international discussions on lunar activity standards.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:01.180
Next steps include public workshops and a report summarizing community recommendations.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:07.020
From policy discussions, we note an exhibition celebrating one nation's lunar milestones.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:13.620
The exhibition marks the anniversary of the UAE's first moon landing attempt and highlights prior

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:19.940
missions. It showcases hardware and data from the Emirates Mars mission alongside lunar efforts.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:25.640
Visitors can explore timelines of the Muhammad bin Rashid Space Center's achievements.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:31.980
The partnership with Omega emphasizes precision timing technology used in space operations.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:37.740
The display runs through the end of the year in the UAE. From national achievements,

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.280
we close with Canada's adjusted lunar robotics plans.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:46.640
The Canadian Space Agency announced plans to redirect resources from the original

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:50.600
Canadarm 3 design toward broader lunar surface operations.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:56.420
MDA Space confirmed it will support the revised approach with updated robotic systems.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:01.720
The change aligns with evolving international plans for sustained presence on the moon.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:07.560
Work will focus on technologies that can operate in the lunar environment for extended periods.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.380
Details on the new architecture are expected in coming months.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:18.000
The ALMA image of the Trillion-mile gas streamer feeding GigaWats Orionis reveals how material

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.200
from a surrounding cloud can reach a young triple-star system and tilt its outer disc.

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:30.220
The narrow stream stretches 0.2 light years, delivering fresh gas that likely torques the planet

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:36.120
forming material out of alignment. This single observation shows external feeding can dominate

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:42.260
disc evolution even after the initial collapse phase. Follow-up observations will track whether

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:45.760
the streamer persists and how much mass it ultimately delivers.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.980
What fraction of other young systems experience similar external tilting events?

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:58.220
A gas streamer, 0.2 light years long, is roughly the distance from the sun to the nearest star system

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:03.700
beyond our own. Yet it remains narrow enough that it could thread between two neighboring solar

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:10.100
systems without touching either. Picture riding alongside one hydrogen molecule as it travels that

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:16.500
entire length. It would take thousands of years at typical cloud speeds, all while staying confined

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:23.140
within a channel only a few hundred astronomical units wide. ALMA's radio observations show this

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:29.800
channel is actively delivering fresh material into the GigaWats Orionis system, where the incoming

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:35.800
gas carries enough angular momentum to twist the outer disc. The mechanism works because the

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:40.600
streamer arrives from a different direction than the original cloud core that formed the stars,

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:47.400
so its momentum vector is misaligned with the existing disc. We can measure the streamer's mass flow

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:53.640
and the resulting warp angle, but nobody yet knows how often such streams occur around other young

00:08:53.640 --> 00:09:00.080
multiples or whether they are the main reason some discs end up tilted. Before we go, keep an

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:06.540
eye on upcoming JWST observations that will test whether the re-ionization pattern holds across

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:13.040
additional fields. That's fascinating frontiers for today. If you enjoyed this, a rating or a

